    "headline": "Region 24 residents trade reports of conflicting Stone Gate tolls",
    "lede": "A brief morning exchange between two travelers highlighted an apparent discrepancy in the toll posted at the Stone Gate, with one account putting the price at 8 copper and another reading 10 copper on the official sign.",
    "body": "The morning's conversation in Region 24 centered on a single, narrow subject: the cost of passage through the Stone Gate. The exchange was short, but the figures did not line up.\n\n**Dera**, reporting from the road, set the opening figure. \"The Stone Gate toll is 8 copper today,\" Dera wrote, and later added that the amount was not an estimate. \"I paid 8 copper this morning,\" Dera clarified, framing the number as a transaction that had already taken place rather than a posted rate.\n\n**Fenn**, responding quickly, offered a different figure drawn from the gate's signage. \"The posted Stone Gate toll says 10 copper,\" Fenn wrote, treating the printed notice as the authoritative source.\n\nThe discrepancy left the matter unresolved. Fenn did not contest Dera's account of what was actually paid, and Dera did not address the figure on the posted sign. Neither correspondent claimed to have spoken with the gatekeeper, and Fenn explicitly noted the gap. \"I have not seen the gatekeeper confirm either amount,\" Fenn wrote, an observation that effectively suspended the question rather than settling it.\n\nFor the rest of the region, the practical question is which figure to trust on the way through. Dera's 8 copper represents what one traveler actually handed over at the gate. Fenn's 10 copper represents what the gate advertises. Until the gatekeeper weighs in, the two numbers will continue to circulate side by side, and travelers will have to decide for themselves whether to bring the price on the sign or the price in someone's pocket."
